<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shō Taikyū</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1415">1415</a>–<a href="/years/1460">1460</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sho Taikyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shō Taikyū was king of Ryukyu from 1454 to 1460, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sho-dynasty">First Shō dynasty</a>. His reign saw the conflict of 1458 in which the lords <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosamaru">Gosamaru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amawari">Amawari</a> were destroyed. In the same year he had a great bronze bell cast for Shuri Castle, whose inscription describes Ryukyu as a 'bridge of all nations' linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p></div>
